Emulate counter reads in User-mode Linux on arm64

Guest instructions run natively in the stub processes, so reads of
CNTVCT_EL0, CNTVCTSS_EL0 and CNTFRQ_EL0 would see the host counter and its
frequency. Each stub enables PR_TSC_SIGSEGV before running guest code, which
needs a host running Linux 6.12 or later. The host delivers the trap as a
SIGSEGV with si_code SI_KERNEL and leaves the signal frame's ESR from an
earlier fault, so the SIGSEGV path decodes the instruction before it
classifies the fault. Counter reads charge CONFIG_HARMONY_UML_CLOCK_READ_VNS
and return the virtual time in nanoseconds, frequency reads return 1 GHz,
and both step over the instruction. Every other fault keeps its signal.
The stub waits for the kernel's reply without the RFC's counter-bounded
spin, since its own counter reads now fault.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a kernel diff under
consonance/harmony-linux/linux/patches/ per the repository's kernel-patch
exception.

